        "central_species": "Fe2+",
        "definition": "A type of molecular magnetism that is the result of electronic instability (see electronic stability) caused by external constraints (temperature, pressure, or electromagnetic radiation), which induce structural changes at molecular and lattice levels. The phenomenon is most characteristic of first-row transition metal complexes, e.g. those of $\\ce{Fe^{2+}}$. An example of spin-crossover complexes (the term of spin-state isomers is also used) is $\\ce{[Fe(2-pic)3]Cl2.EtOH}$ (2-pic = 2-picolylamine). At the $\\ce{Fe–N}$ distance of \\(203.2\\ \\rm{pm}\\) (\\(115\\ \\rm{K}\\)), the complex has an electronic low-spin state ($^{1}{\\rm{A}}_{1}$), whereas stretching the bond up to \\(219.9\\ \\rm{pm}\\) at \\(227\\ \\rm{K}\\) induces the transition to a high-spin state ($^{5}{\\rm{T}}_{2}$).",
